  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nA few minor precip chances over the next week, otherwise quiet \nweather with temperatures at or above normal.\n\nFreezing rain tonight...Models continue to show a small potential\n(15-35% chance) for light freezing rain tonight as a weak short-\nwave and surface trough generate a narrow band a precip moving \nacross WI from west to east. Forecast soundings in central and \nnorth- central WI show a 2 to 4 hour window with deeper \nsaturation, a warm nose from about 1-4kft, and sub-freezing \nsurface temps. While the temperature profile would make freezing \nrain the dominate ptype, the main question is weather any \nprecipitation will reach the ground in the first place. Overall \nlift is weak, and may not line up well with the limited moisture, \nleading to drying. While only a couple hundredths of QPF is \nanticipated (probabilities for 0.01\" of QPF sit at 15-35%), any \namount of QPF/ice would create slippery spots on roads. Coverage \nof ice will be spotty and short lived as temperatures Saturday \nmorning rise above freezing.\n\nAnyone driving late tonight and early Saturday should take extra \ncaution and remain aware of the potential for icy patches. Best \ntiming and location is between 2:00 to 8:00 AM for central and \nnorth-central WI. Temperatures farther east are less likely to be\nbelow freezing, especially by the time the precip line reaches \nthis area. \n\nSunday precip and beyond...The precipitation potential for Sunday\nhas come down significantly over the past couple days as northern\nand southern stream systems pass too far north/south. Most places\nwill stay dry on Sunday. There remains a small (15-25%) chance \nfor light snow in northern Vilas County, and rain in east- central\nWI on Sunday. High pressure builds in the Ohio River Valley Mon-\nTue resulting in dry weather for Wisconsin. Flow becomes more \nprogressive towards the end of next week, with various waves \npotentially bringing rain or mixed rain/snow, but it is too early \nto determine any more specific details. \n\nTemperatures...Readings will be near normal from Sunday through\nTuesday morning, with morning lows falling into the mid teens to\nlow 20s, and highs ranging from 30 to 40 degrees. The rest of the\nperiod will be above normal.",
